## 2. Executive Verdict

`ACCEPT_TWO_TRANCHE_DIRECTION` with mandatory pre-R1A corrections. The
redesign correctly diagnoses and structurally repairs the rejected P3
defect (an allowlist comparison masquerading as an oracle-bound replay,
independently confirmed by direct inspection of the `.rejected` helper's
`_evaluate_case` function, which contains no import of, or call into, either
P2 seam module). Two execution tranches plus one non-executable umbrella is
the minimum structure that separates "who defines expected truth" from "who
observes actual behavior." However, three of the fourteen review-question
areas (oracle-to-seam causal binding at the *predicate* level, the ledger's
own freeze/mutation discipline, and reviewer verification cost) are specified
at the principle level but leave enough implementation freedom that an R1A
manifest or R1B runner could satisfy every named contract literally while
reproducing a materially similar defect one layer down. These are named as
concrete failure modes below and must be closed in the R1A deliverable and
R1B work order, not deferred to reviewer discretion after the fact.

